portant Intangible Folk Cultural Properties:row:5", "relation": "remarks", "tail": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "source_type": "table", "source_id": "List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties", "row_index": 5, "column_name": "Remarks", "header_path": ["Remarks"], "structural": true, "traversal_from": "table:List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties:row:5", "traversal_to": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "direction": "forward"}, {"edge_id": "record:210", "head": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "relation": "linked_passage", "tail": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "source_type": "table", "source_id": "List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties", "row_index": 5, "column_name": "Remarks", "header_path": ["Remarks"], "structural": true, "contexts": [{"source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "text": "In Japan , Daikokuten ( 大黒天 ) , the god of great darkness or blackness , or the god of five cereals , is one of the Seven Lucky Gods ( Fukujin ) . Daikokuten evolved from the Buddhist form of the Indian deity Shiva intertwined with the Shinto god Ōkuninushi . The name is the Japanese equivalent of Mahākāla , the Hindu name for Shiva . The god enjoys an exalted position as a household deity in Japan . Daikoku 's association with wealth and prosperity precipitated a custom known as fukunusubi , or theft of fortune . This custom started with the belief that whoever stole divine figures was assured of good fortune if not caught in the act . The toshi-no-ichi ( year-end market ) held at Sensō-ji became the main venue of the sale and disposal of such images by the fortune-seekers . Many small stalls were opened where articles including images of Daikoku were sold on the eve of New Year celebrations . The Japanese also maintain the symbol of Mahakala as a monogram . The traditional pilgrims climbing the holy Mount Ontake wear tenugui ( a kind of white scarf ) with the seed syllable of Mahakala . Daikoku is variously considered to be the god of wealth , or of the household , particularly the kitchen . He is recognised by his wide face , smile , and a flat black hat .", "context_before": "Ojiya ( 小千谷市 , Ojiya-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 34,704 in 12,758 households , and a population density of 224 persons per km² . The total area of the city was 155.19 square kilometres ( 59.92 sq mi ) . Uonuma ( 魚沼市 , Uonuma-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 35,027 in 13,289 households , and a population density of 37 persons per km² . Its total area is 946.76 square kilometres ( 365.55 sq mi ) . The city is famous for its koshihikari rice , which commands a premium in the Japanese market ."}], "traversal_from": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "traversal_to": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "direction": "forward"}], "steps": [{"step": 1, "goal": "Tsuruga Nishimachi tug of war ( 敦賀西町の綱引き , Tsuruga Nishimachi no tsuna-hiki ) --has_record--> table:List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties:row:5", "depends_on": null}, {"step": 2, "goal": "table:List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties:row:5 --remarks--> Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "depends_on": 1}, {"step": 3, "goal": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins --linked_passage--> passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "depends_on": 2}], "path_verification": {"status": "qualified", "hard_failures": [], "question_term_coverage": 0.5833, "table_witness_indices": [], "linked_witness": false, "numeric_evidence": null, "terminal_is_container": true, "selection_score": 11.9111}}, {"path_id": "grounded_76", "score": 6.7611, "score_components": {"grounded_edges": 1.0, "query_entity_match": 0.5, "relation_match": 0.0, "text_context_match": 1.0, "query_coverage": 1.1111, "single_venue_specificity": 0.0, "constraint_coverage": 0.0, "cross_modal_completeness": 1.5, "row_context": 0.0, "row_entity_binding": 0.0, "numeric_literal_coverage": 0.0, "strong_keyword_coverage": 0.0, "hyperlink_passage_bridge": 2.0, "table_witness_match": 0.0, "answer_type_path_filter": 0.0, "same_fact_corroboration": 0.0, "length_penalty": 0.35}, "nodes": ["city", "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins"], "edges": [{"edge_id": "record:275", "head": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "relation": "mentions", "tail": "city", "source_type": "text", "source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "row_index": null, "column_name": null, "header_path": null, "structural": true, "contexts": [{"source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "text": "In Japan , Daikokuten ( 大黒天 ) , the god of great darkness or blackness , or the god of five cereals , is one of the Seven Lucky Gods ( Fukujin ) . Daikokuten evolved from the Buddhist form of the Indian deity Shiva intertwined with the Shinto god Ōkuninushi . The name is the Japanese equivalent of Mahākāla , the Hindu name for Shiva . The god enjoys an exalted position as a household deity in Japan . Daikoku 's association with wealth and prosperity precipitated a custom known as fukunusubi , or theft of fortune . This custom started with the belief that whoever stole divine figures was assured of good fortune if not caught in the act . The toshi-no-ichi ( year-end market ) held at Sensō-ji became the main venue of the sale and disposal of such images by the fortune-seekers . Many small stalls were opened where articles including images of Daikoku were sold on the eve of New Year celebrations . The Japanese also maintain the symbol of Mahakala as a monogram . The traditional pilgrims climbing the holy Mount Ontake wear tenugui ( a kind of white scarf ) with the seed syllable of Mahakala . Daikoku is variously considered to be the god of wealth , or of the household , particularly the kitchen . He is recognised by his wide face , smile , and a flat black hat .", "context_before": "Ojiya ( 小千谷市 , Ojiya-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 34,704 in 12,758 households , and a population density of 224 persons per km² . The total area of the city was 155.19 square kilometres ( 59.92 sq mi ) . Uonuma ( 魚沼市 , Uonuma-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 35,027 in 13,289 households , and a population density of 37 persons per km² . Its total area is 946.76 square kilometres ( 365.55 sq mi ) . The city is famous for its koshihikari rice , which commands a premium in the Japanese market ."}], "traversal_from": "city", "traversal_to": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "direction": "reverse"}, {"edge_id": "record:210", "head": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "relation": "linked_passage", "tail": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "source_type": "table", "source_id": "List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties", "row_index": 5, "column_name": "Remarks", "header_path": ["Remarks"], "structural": true, "contexts": [{"source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "text": "In Japan , Daikokuten ( 大黒天 ) , the god of great darkness or blackness , or the god of five cereals , is one of the Seven Lucky Gods ( Fukujin ) . Daikokuten evolved from the Buddhist form of the Indian deity Shiva intertwined with the Shinto god Ōkuninushi . The name is the Japanese equivalent of Mahākāla , the Hindu name for Shiva . The god enjoys an exalted position as a household deity in Japan . Daikoku 's association with wealth and prosperity precipitated a custom known as fukunusubi , or theft of fortune . This custom started with the belief that whoever stole divine figures was assured of good fortune if not caught in the act . The toshi-no-ichi ( year-end market ) held at Sensō-ji became the main venue of the sale and disposal of such images by the fortune-seekers . Many small stalls were opened where articles including images of Daikoku were sold on the eve of New Year celebrations . The Japanese also maintain the symbol of Mahakala as a monogram . The traditional pilgrims climbing the holy Mount Ontake wear tenugui ( a kind of white scarf ) with the seed syllable of Mahakala . Daikoku is variously considered to be the god of wealth , or of the household , particularly the kitchen . He is recognised by his wide face , smile , and a flat black hat .", "context_before": "Ojiya ( 小千谷市 , Ojiya-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 34,704 in 12,758 households , and a population density of 224 persons per km² . The total area of the city was 155.19 square kilometres ( 59.92 sq mi ) . Uonuma ( 魚沼市 , Uonuma-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 35,027 in 13,289 households , and a population density of 37 persons per km² . Its total area is 946.76 square kilometres ( 365.55 sq mi ) . The city is famous for its koshihikari rice , which commands a premium in the Japanese market ."}], "traversal_from": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "traversal_to": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins", "direction": "reverse"}], "steps": [{"step": 1, "goal": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku --mentions--> city [traverse reverse]", "depends_on": null}, {"step": 2, "goal": "Tug of war between two teams representing Daikoku and Ebisu . The year is said to bring a good harvest if Daikoku wins , and a good catch if Ebisu wins --linked_passage--> passage:/wiki/Daikoku [traverse reverse]", "depends_on": 1}], "path_verification": {"status": "qualified", "hard_failures": [], "question_term_coverage": 0.5, "table_witness_indices": [], "linked_witness": false, "numeric_evidence": null, "terminal_is_container": false, "selection_score": 11.7611}}, {"path_id": "grounded_83", "score": 6.7611, "score_components": {"grounded_edges": 1.0, "query_entity_match": 0.5, "relation_match": 0.0, "text_context_match": 1.0, "query_coverage": 1.1111, "single_venue_specificity": 0.0, "constraint_coverage": 0.0, "cross_modal_completeness": 1.5, "row_context": 0.0, "row_entity_binding": 0.0, "numeric_literal_coverage": 0.0, "strong_keyword_coverage": 0.0, "hyperlink_passage_bridge": 2.0, "table_witness_match": 0.0, "answer_type_path_filter": 0.0, "same_fact_corroboration": 0.0, "length_penalty": 0.35}, "nodes": ["city", "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "Daikoku"], "edges": [{"edge_id": "record:275", "head": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "relation": "mentions", "tail": "city", "source_type": "text", "source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "row_index": null, "column_name": null, "header_path": null, "structural": true, "contexts": [{"source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "text": "In Japan , Daikokuten ( 大黒天 ) , the god of great darkness or blackness , or the god of five cereals , is one of the Seven Lucky Gods ( Fukujin ) . Daikokuten evolved from the Buddhist form of the Indian deity Shiva intertwined with the Shinto god Ōkuninushi . The name is the Japanese equivalent of Mahākāla , the Hindu name for Shiva . The god enjoys an exalted position as a household deity in Japan . Daikoku 's association with wealth and prosperity precipitated a custom known as fukunusubi , or theft of fortune . This custom started with the belief that whoever stole divine figures was assured of good fortune if not caught in the act . The toshi-no-ichi ( year-end market ) held at Sensō-ji became the main venue of the sale and disposal of such images by the fortune-seekers . Many small stalls were opened where articles including images of Daikoku were sold on the eve of New Year celebrations . The Japanese also maintain the symbol of Mahakala as a monogram . The traditional pilgrims climbing the holy Mount Ontake wear tenugui ( a kind of white scarf ) with the seed syllable of Mahakala . Daikoku is variously considered to be the god of wealth , or of the household , particularly the kitchen . He is recognised by his wide face , smile , and a flat black hat .", "context_before": "Ojiya ( 小千谷市 , Ojiya-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 34,704 in 12,758 households , and a population density of 224 persons per km² . The total area of the city was 155.19 square kilometres ( 59.92 sq mi ) . Uonuma ( 魚沼市 , Uonuma-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 35,027 in 13,289 households , and a population density of 37 persons per km² . Its total area is 946.76 square kilometres ( 365.55 sq mi ) . The city is famous for its koshihikari rice , which commands a premium in the Japanese market ."}], "traversal_from": "city", "traversal_to": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "direction": "reverse"}, {"edge_id": "record:211", "head": "Daikoku", "relation": "linked_passage", "tail": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "source_type": "table", "source_id": "List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties", "row_index": 5, "column_name": "Remarks", "header_path": ["Remarks"], "structural": true, "derived_from_cell_split": true, "contexts": [{"source_id": "/wiki/Daikoku", "text": "In Japan , Daikokuten ( 大黒天 ) , the god of great darkness or blackness , or the god of five cereals , is one of the Seven Lucky Gods ( Fukujin ) . Daikokuten evolved from the Buddhist form of the Indian deity Shiva intertwined with the Shinto god Ōkuninushi . The name is the Japanese equivalent of Mahākāla , the Hindu name for Shiva . The god enjoys an exalted position as a household deity in Japan . Daikoku 's association with wealth and prosperity precipitated a custom known as fukunusubi , or theft of fortune . This custom started with the belief that whoever stole divine figures was assured of good fortune if not caught in the act . The toshi-no-ichi ( year-end market ) held at Sensō-ji became the main venue of the sale and disposal of such images by the fortune-seekers . Many small stalls were opened where articles including images of Daikoku were sold on the eve of New Year celebrations . The Japanese also maintain the symbol of Mahakala as a monogram . The traditional pilgrims climbing the holy Mount Ontake wear tenugui ( a kind of white scarf ) with the seed syllable of Mahakala . Daikoku is variously considered to be the god of wealth , or of the household , particularly the kitchen . He is recognised by his wide face , smile , and a flat black hat .", "context_before": "Ojiya ( 小千谷市 , Ojiya-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 34,704 in 12,758 households , and a population density of 224 persons per km² . The total area of the city was 155.19 square kilometres ( 59.92 sq mi ) . Uonuma ( 魚沼市 , Uonuma-shi ) is a city located in Niigata Prefecture , Japan . As of 1 July 2019 [ update ] , the city had an estimated population of 35,027 in 13,289 households , and a population density of 37 persons per km² . Its total area is 946.76 square kilometres ( 365.55 sq mi ) . The city is famous for its koshihikari rice , which commands a premium in the Japanese market ."}], "traversal_from": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku", "traversal_to": "Daikoku", "direction": "reverse"}], "steps": [{"step": 1, "goal": "passage:/wiki/Daikoku --mentions--> city [traverse reverse]", "depends_on": null}, {"step": 2, "goal": "Daikoku --linked_passage--> passage:/wiki/Daikoku [traverse reverse]", "depends_on": 1}], "path_verification": {"status": "qualified", "hard_failures": [], "question_term_coverage": 0.5, "table_witness_indices": [], "linked_witness": false, "numeric_evidence": null, "terminal_is_container": false, "selection_score": 11.7611}}, {"path_id": "row_4_22", "score": 7.2167, "score_components": {"grounded_edges": 1.0, "query_entity_match": 0.0, "relation_match": 0.0, "text_context_match": 0.75, "query_coverage": 0.6667, "single_venue_specificity": 0.0, "constraint_coverage": 0.0, "cross_modal_completeness": 1.5, "row_context": 1.0, "row_entity_binding": 1.0, "numeric_literal_coverage": 0.0, "strong_keyword_coverage": 0.0, "hyperlink_passage_bridge": 2.0, "table_witness_match": 0.0, "answer_type_path_filter": 0.0, "same_fact_corroboration": 0.0, "length_penalty": 0.7}, "nodes": ["Bull wrestling ( 牛の角突きの習俗 , Ushi no tsunotsuki no shūzoku )", "table:List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties:row:4", "Nagaoka , Ojiya and Uonuma , Niigata", "passage:/wiki/Uonuma,_Niigata"], "edges": [{"edge_id": "record:57", "head": "Bull wrestling ( 牛の角突きの習俗 , Ushi no tsunotsuki no shūzoku )", "relation": "has_record", "tail": "table:List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties:row:4", "source_type": "table", "source_id": "List of Important Intangible Folk Cultural Properties", "row_index": 4, "column_name": "Name", "header_path": ["Name"], "structural": true, "traversal_from": "Bull wrestling ( 牛の角突きの習俗 , Ushi no tsunotsuki no shūzoku )", "traversal_